<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">España Boba</h1><p class="st-meta">1809 CE – 1821 CE · capital Santo Domingo</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The period of restored but neglectful Spanish rule over Santo Domingo, nicknamed 'Foolish Spain', which ended with a brief declaration of independence.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Dominican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/criollo-people">creoles</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-sanchez-ramirez">Juan Sánchez Ramírez</a> expelled the French from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in 1809, with British help, and voluntarily returned the colony to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. Spain itself was then fighting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, and the restored colony received little money, troops or attention.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-sanchez-ramirez">Sánchez Ramírez</a> governed until his death in 1811, followed by captains general such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-de-urrutia">Carlos de Urrutia</a>, mocked as 'Carlos Conuco' for making soldiers farm, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebastian-kindelan">Sebastián Kindelán</a>. The economy, already ruined by war and emigration, depended on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, tobacco and mahogany, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paper-money">paper money</a> lost value. Conspiracies included the so-called Italian plot of 1810 and a slave revolt plan of 1812, both harshly suppressed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain's</a> liberal constitution of 1812 was briefly applied.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Disillusioned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, the lawyer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-nunez-de-caceres">José Núñez de Cáceres</a> proclaimed independence on 1 December 1821 as the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/republic-of-spanish-haiti">Republic of Spanish Haiti</a>. The colony's weakness made the Haitian occupation of 1822 easy.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/espana-boba.jpg" alt="España Boba" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: previous version Ignaciogavira; current version HansenBCN, designs from SanchoPanzaXXI · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Spain_(1785%E2%80%931873,_1875%E2%80%931931).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1809 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santo Domingo</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Juan Sánchez Ramírez, Sebastián Kindelán</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Spanish Santo Domingo (1809-1821)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=espana-boba" aria-label="España Boba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2785 1493 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where España Boba lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3105 1749L3105 1749M3105 1749L4910 989M3105 1749L3105 1749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3105 1749h0M4910 989h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3105 1749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Santo Domingo. <a href="/atlas?year=1815&amp;focus=espana-boba">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
